<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Toltec state</h1><p class="st-meta">900 CE – 1150 CE (approx.) · capital Tula</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Early Postclassic power centred on Tula in Hidalgo, remembered by the Aztecs as the model of civilisation, artistry and legitimate kingship.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Tula, or Tollan, grew in the 9th century in the valley of the Tula river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hidalgo-state">Hidalgo</a>, settled by migrants from the north and from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valley-of-mexico">Valley of Mexico</a>. By about 900 it had become the largest city of central <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mexico">Mexico</a>, with a population of perhaps 40,000 or more.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tula-mesoamerican-site">Tula Grande</a>, the ceremonial core, held Pyramid B crowned by colossal stone warrior figures (the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantes-of-tula">Atlanteans</a>), colonnaded halls, ballcourts and a skull rack. Its art stressed military orders, feathered serpents and sacrifice. Obsidian from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pachuca">Pachuca</a> was a key resource. Later Aztec traditions told of the priest-king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/topiltzin-quetzalcoatl">Ce Acatl Topiltzin Quetzalcoatl</a> and the last ruler Huemac, but these accounts are legendary.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Tula was burnt and its rule collapsed around 1150, and its people dispersed. Later dynasties such as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/culhuacan">Culhuacan</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire</a> claimed Toltec descent to legitimise their power, making 'Toltec' a byword for skilled craftsmen.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/toltec-state.jpg" alt="Toltec state" width="320" height="238"><figcaption>Image: Mabarlabin · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Toltec_influence.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>900 CE – 1150 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tula</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Mesoamerican religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Probably Nahuatl</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Toltec Empire, Tula, Tollan, Toltecs</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1025&amp;focus=toltec-state" aria-label="Toltec state on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2097 1488 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Toltec state lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2417 1744h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2417 1744h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Tula. <a href="/atlas?year=1025&amp;focus=toltec-state">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
